<PageLayout
  title="Victim Advocacy Education & Legislative Reform | Silver Lining of Hope"
  description="Community education and legislative reform for victims' rights in Indiana — including Dom & Davion's Law and Hailey's Law — from Silver Lining of Hope."
  heading="Education & reform"
  intro="Individual cases get solved. Systems get reformed. We work on both."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Programs', href: '/programs' },
    { label: 'Education & reform', href: '/programs/education-reform' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="container--narrow section prose">
    <p>
      The "E" in HOPE stands for education and reform. Every case we work
      teaches us where the system failed: a report that wasn't taken
      seriously, a database entry that was never made, a death ruling that
      closed doors instead of opening them, a family that didn't know their
      rights because no one told them. This pillar turns those lessons into
      public education and concrete legislative change, so the next family
      faces fewer of the obstacles the last one did.
    </p>

    <h2>Education: what we teach</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>For families and communities:</strong> what to do in the
        first 48 hours of a disappearance, how public records laws work in
        Indiana, how to document domestic violence safely, and how to
        advocate for a case without harming it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>For advocates and volunteers:</strong> trauma-informed
        communication, responsible research methods, and the ethics of
        working on real cases involving real grief — the opposite of
        true-crime spectacle.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>For professionals and students:</strong> presentations on
        hidden homicides, missing person data gaps, and what our founder's
        seven months of independent research revealed about how cases stall
        — and how they restart. <a href="/get-involved/request-a-speaker">Request
        a speaker</a> for your agency, class, or organization.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Reform: the laws we fight for</h2>
    <p>
      We support and advocate for Indiana legislation that protects victims
      and closes the gaps our casework exposes, including:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Dom &amp; Davion's Law</strong> — strengthening protections
        for children in custody and welfare situations where warning signs
        went unheeded.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hailey's Law</strong> — improving how law enforcement
        responds to and tracks missing person reports.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Death investigation standards</strong> — pushing for better
        training, documentation, and review when deaths are ruled accidental
        or undetermined despite unresolved questions.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Follow the current bills, sign active petitions, and find your
      legislators on our
      <a href="/get-involved/legislative-advocacy">legislative advocacy
      page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Why reform belongs in a casework organization</h2>
    <p>
      Advocacy organizations that only work cases treat symptoms. The
      families we serve through our
      <a href="/programs/homicides">homicide review</a> and
      <a href="/programs/missing-persons">missing persons</a> programs
      deserve more than sympathy for what went wrong — they deserve an
      organization that carries their experience into hearing rooms where the
      rules get rewritten. When a family tells us their story, part of our
      promise is that it will count for something beyond their own case.
    </p>

    <h2>Get involved</h2>
    <p>
      Education and reform run on volunteers: researchers who track bills,
      writers who translate legal language into plain English, and advocates
      who show up at the Statehouse.
      <a href="/get-involved/volunteer">Join us</a>, or
      <a href="/donate">fund the work</a> — every training we give and every
      bill we support is free to the public because donors make it so.
    </p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
